<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cuenca, Ecuador</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cuenca is the third-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and capital of Azuay province, in the southern highlands. It stands on the site of the Cañari centre of Guapondelig and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomebamba">Tomebamba</a>, and was founded as a Spanish city in 1557. Its well-preserved colonial centre is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cuenca-ecuador.jpg" alt="Cuenca, Ecuador" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David C. S. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Collage_Cuenca.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cuenca lies in the south-central inter-Andean region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>, in the Paute river basin, at about 2,538 metres above sea level, roughly 470 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> and 200 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a>. Four rivers cross it, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomebamba">Tomebamba</a>, Yanuncay, Tarqui and Machángara, and their confluence gives the city its Spanish name, which means a basin. The first three rise in the páramo of El Cajas National Park to the west. Its full name, Santa Ana de los Ríos de Cuenca, honours Saint Anne and the Spanish city of Cuenca, home of the viceroy who ordered its founding.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological finds trace the first inhabitants in the area to about 8060 BCE, in the Cave of Chopsi, where nomadic hunters followed the animals. Later peoples grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a>, quinoa and squash and kept guinea pigs, llamas and alpacas. From around 2000 BCE society became more organised, with specialised administrative and religious authorities, and later came the Cañari, who were absorbed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> in the 15th century. Archaeologists believe a Cañari settlement called Guapdondelic, said to mean land as big as heaven, was founded around 500 CE.</p><p>After a bitter struggle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> conquered the Cañari, likely in the 1470s. They replaced the Cañari architecture with their own but did not suppress the Cañari or their achievements in astronomy and agriculture, and they renamed the city <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomebamba">Tomebamba</a>. The emperor Tupac Yupanqui ordered the building of a grand city called Pumapungo, said to rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, and Tomebamba became known as the second Cusco. When the Spaniards found it, they saw only ruins, and it was sparsely populated until the 1550s.</p><p>Gil Ramírez Dávalos founded the Spanish city on 12 April 1557 on the orders of the viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, Andrés Hurtado de Mendoza, decades after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> (1534), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> (1538) and Loja (1548). The city achieved independence on 3 November 1820 and reached the peak of its importance in the first years of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>, as the capital of one of the republic's three provinces, with Guayaquil and Quito.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cuenca is the capital of Azuay province and one of the largest cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>. Its historic centre was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1999. The Old Cathedral, built in 1557, is now a museum, and the New Cathedral, completed in 1975, has blue and white domes that symbolise the city. The Universidad de Cuenca, founded in 1867, is among the oldest universities in the country. Cuenca is known for its textiles and for the straw hats called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> hats, which are made there by local artisans.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cuenca, Ecuador” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their chiefdoms formed a loose confederation, with Guapondelig, near modern <b>Cuenca</b>, as a leading centre and Hatun Cañar, now Ingapirca, as another.</blockquote><a href="/state/canari-confederation">Cañari Confederation</a></li><li><blockquote>Bitter rivalry between the Montúfar faction and the Sánchez de Orellana family weakened the government, and <b>Cuenca</b> and Guayaquil remained royalist.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-quito">State of Quito</a></li></ul></section>
